<p>There’s the “caprice of the gods,” with which every religion has had to deal, as well as the even more universal feeling of nostalgia, “a very overused and sentimentalized term now, which literally means — and can only really mean — the yearning to go home.” Adducing such re-interpretations by writers from Alexander Pope to James Joyce, Hitchens declares that we think of the <em>Odyssey&nbsp;</em>as “the original narrative of the striving of humanity — and of the need, also, to beware of what you want, in case you ever do, <a href="https://www.poetryfoundation.org/poems/51296/ithaka-56d22eef917ec">as Cavafy says</a>, finally get home to Ithaca. It may turn out that it’s better to travel hopefully than to arrive.”</p>
<p>The matter of virtue also arises in the conversation, and specifically whether the range of virtues of which Homer sang (up to and including luck) would be recognized in our Christian or post-Christian world. To the avowedly atheist Hitchens’ mind, of course, we should already have been post-religious. Homer’s deities are “anthropomorphic gods; they are invented by us, as all gods are.” But whatever your religion or lack thereof, “if you don’t know something about classical Greece, then you won’t be able, with much profit, to read Shakespeare, or Milton, or Shelley, or Keats, or even Chaucer. It would be like not knowing what the bible story was and then wondering how you puzzle your way through Shakespeare as well. It’s part of the underlay; it’s part of the common heritage.” The translations and re-interpretations have kept coming, but Homer himself still patiently awaits each of us.</p>
